  "args": "{\"description\":\"MEASURED where logue's memory actually goes (peak footprint via /usr/bin/time -l, zig-out birnam): bare birnam 679MB; filing in logue-core.birnam (90 nested-closure forms) = 3357MB (~2.7GB delta, ~30MB PER top-level form); +1 dungeon generation +23MB; +2000 turns of play +260MB. So reification is dominated by LOADING THE PROGRAM, not runtime state: every top-level let asserts a scope-binding fact (scopeBindValueImpl-\\u003ecAssertFact) and each closure's AST nodes + bytecode method are reified as entities (code-is-facts: AST-as-entities + BYTECODE_METHOD_VT). ~30MB/form is super-linear write amplification — ADR-perf-1 finding #1 (5-index path-copy + never-freed history) applied to the file-in form loop, no GC/prune mid-fileIn. CONSEQUENCE: large birnam SOURCE files are expensive to LOAD (GBs, ~10s), independent of workload. retainCurrent does NOT help: it only set_retention(0,0) (policy floor, no compaction is birnam-exposed) AND the bulk isn't runtime history anyway. Real levers: load code without per-AST reification, mmap'd AOT image (12.5 deferred), or smaller programs.\",\"kind\":\"bug\",\"scope\":\"birnam:examples/logue\",\"source\":\"chazu\",\"status\":\"raw\",\"worth\":0.5}",
