  "id": "5e9542d731956ab9d6678054edf5e50ce629f0c30bd071416dd9b20cfb23081c",
  "relation": "observation",
  "args": "{\"description\":\"Binding nil to a TOP-LEVEL/scope 'let' hard-panics the kernel: 'FactStore.assert: NIL value forbidden (Date discipline)' via lang.scopeBindValueImpl -\\u003e pushScopeBinding -\\u003e cAssertFact. It is an uncatchable native panic (not an ERROR_VALUE), aborting the whole process — so 'let x := [monsterAt ...]' at top level crashes when the lookup returns nil. Block-LOCAL lets (frame slots inside a method/block) hold nil fine; only top-level/REPL scope bindings get reified as facts and trip the no-nil rule. Practical rule for scripts/REPL: never 'let' a possibly-nil expression at top level — guard with ifTrue:ifFalse: or keep it block-local. Ideally the binding path should surface a catchable error or allow nil at the scope layer, not panic.\",\"kind\":\"bug\",\"scope\":\"birnam:examples/logue\",\"source\":\"chazu\",\"status\":\"raw\",\"worth\":0.5}",
  "valid_start": 1782262975,
