{
  "id": "afc3f413ae220f1174701f64d595d09133b841d509f5b6d95f940372bcea1a47",
  "relation": "observation",
  "args": "{\"description\":\"Birnam gotcha (3rd of the bracket family): wrapping a BARE variable in brackets — '[crit]', '[adj]', '[MAP_W]' — as a receiver errors. '[[crit] ifTrue:...]' fails; the variable must be the receiver directly: '[crit ifTrue:...]'. Brackets '[...]' are a message-send/grouping that REQUIRES a selector or a real sub-send inside; '[bareIdentifier]' alone is not valid (no message). Same root cause as the redundant-send-wrap: '[ X ]' is only legal when X is 'receiver selector: args', a literal, or a block — never a lone variable. These three bracket gotchas (cell-rejects-nil aside) — redundant send wrap, bare-var wrap, and miscount — were the bulk of the debugging cost writing a large birnam program by hand, made worse by fileIn reporting only an error COUNT, not locations.\",\"kind\":\"antipattern\",\"scope\":\"birnam:examples/logue\",\"source\":\"chazu\",\"status\":\"raw\",\"worth\":0.5}",
  "valid_start": 1782262455,
  "tx_start": 1782262455,
