{
  "id": "8b09d4a3c61c5c8af9907e74bf86c2754f5c1cdee27f7f5fa71fbfbe462979c0",
  "relation": "observation",
  "args": "{\"description\":\"Birnam gotcha that cost real debugging time: wrapping a COMPLETE message-send in redundant outer brackets — '[[m at: #k put: v]]' or '[[[[chain]]]]' with one extra level — is NOT a no-op grouping; it raises a runtime error (outer '[ ]' has a receiver but no selector). It compiles/parses (brackets balance) so it slips past the linter, then the enclosing 'let f := [...]' fails to bind, surfacing later as a misleading 'undefined variable: f' at the call site (NOT at the definition). Rule: every '[...]' must be a message send '[recv sel: arg]', a literal, or a block — never a grouping wrapper around an already-complete '[...]'. An at:put: chain of N puts needs exactly N+ (receiver) brackets, not N+1.\",\"kind\":\"antipattern\",\"scope\":\"birnam:examples/logue\",\"source\":\"chazu\",\"status\":\"raw\",\"worth\":0.5}",
  "valid_start": 1782262303,
  "tx_start": 1782262303,
